.TH FETESTEXCEPT 3 "2026-05-03" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
fetestexcept \- test floating-point exception flags
.SH LIBRARY
Math Library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fenv.h>
.PP
.BI "int fetestexcept(int " excepts );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR fetestexcept ()
function tests the current floating-point exception sticky flags.
It returns the bitwise AND of the current exception flags and
.IR excepts ,
allowing the caller to determine which of the requested flags are set.
.PP
The argument
.I excepts
is a bitwise OR of zero or more of the following macros:
.TP
.B FE_INVALID
Test the invalid-operation flag.
.TP
.B FE_DIVBYZERO
Test the division-by-zero flag.
.TP
.B FE_OVERFLOW
Test the overflow flag.
.TP
.B FE_UNDERFLOW
Test the underflow flag.
.TP
.B FE_INEXACT
Test the inexact-result flag.
.TP
.B FE_ALL_EXCEPT
Test all exception flags.
.PP
Passing zero for
.I excepts
always returns zero.
.PP
.BR fetestexcept ()
does not clear or modify any flags.
To clear flags after testing them, call
.BR feclearexcept (3).
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR fetestexcept ()
returns the value of the currently set exception flags that are also set in
.IR excepts .
That is, it returns
.IR excepts
masked to only those bits that are currently raised.
Returns zero if none of the requested flags are set.
.SH ERRORS
No errors are defined.
The function does not set
.IR errno .
.SH EXAMPLES
The following example performs a division and then tests whether any
exception flags were raised:
.PP
.nf
#pragma STDC FENV_ACCESS ON
#include <fenv.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    /* Clear all flags before the operation. */
    feclearexcept(FE_ALL_EXCEPT);

    volatile double zero = 0.0;
    volatile double x = 1.0 / zero;   /* raises FE_DIVBYZERO */
    (void)x;

    /* Test for specific flags. */
    int raised = fetestexcept(FE_ALL_EXCEPT);

    if (raised & FE_DIVBYZERO)
        printf("FE_DIVBYZERO raised.\\n");
    if (raised & FE_INVALID)
        printf("FE_INVALID raised.\\n");
    if (raised & FE_OVERFLOW)
        printf("FE_OVERFLOW raised.\\n");
    if (raised & FE_UNDERFLOW)
        printf("FE_UNDERFLOW raised.\\n");
    if (raised & FE_INEXACT)
        printf("FE_INEXACT raised.\\n");

    feclearexcept(FE_ALL_EXCEPT);
    return 0;
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fenv (7),
.BR feclearexcept (3),
.BR feraiseexcept (3)
